{
  "unit": "resample__PMC11375712__broad",
  "auditor": "trapi:gpt-5.6-sol_2026-07-09",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "The material family of interest is fcc high-entropy alloys derived from the Fe-Co-Ni system, specifically CoCrFeMnNi (the Cantor alloy).",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::001::introduction::2dc33c3a9e"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "The targeted area for improvement and understanding is finite-temperature magnetic behavior.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::000::abstract::b3f2cb8881",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::001::introduction::2dc33c3a9e"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Specific target properties include magnetic moments and fcc-hcp phase stability.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::000::abstract::b3f2cb8881",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::002::results_and_discussion::13b860da4c",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::003::methods::f3287d9a39"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "The primary scientific challenge is an incomplete theoretical description of the orbital-moment contribution to the total magnetic moment in the paramagnetic state.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Current first-principles approaches such as DFT with DLM often neglect or inadequately treat orbital moments.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Spin-only models fail to fully capture temperature-dependent longitudinal spin fluctuations.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::001::introduction::2dc33c3a9e",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::002::results_and_discussion::13b860da4c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "The modeled initial state is fcc CoCrFeMnNi in the finite-temperature paramagnetic state.",
      "field": "material_initial_state",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::000::abstract::b3f2cb8881",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::001::introduction::2dc33c3a9e"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "The intervention applies first-principles alloy theory that accounts for longitudinal spin fluctuations and thermal lattice expansion.",
      "field": "intervention",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::000::abstract::b3f2cb8881",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::002::results_and_discussion::13b860da4c",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::003::methods::f3287d9a39"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "The modeled temperature interval is 50 K to 1200 K.",
      "field": "conditions",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::002::results_and_discussion::13b860da4c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Magnetic moments are modeled as functions of temperature and volume represented by the Wigner-Seitz radius.",
      "field": "conditions",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::002::results_and_discussion::13b860da4c",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::003::methods::f3287d9a39"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Longitudinal spin fluctuations induce sizable finite magnetic moments for Co, Cr, and Ni.",
      "field": "structural_mediator",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::000::abstract::b3f2cb8881",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::002::results_and_discussion::13b860da4c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Longitudinal spin fluctuations modify the finite-temperature magnetic moments of Fe and Mn.",
      "field": "structural_mediator",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::002::results_and_discussion::13b860da4c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Longitudinal spin fluctuations affect phase stability, stacking-fault energy, and fcc-hcp interfacial energy.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::000::abstract::b3f2cb8881",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::003::methods::f3287d9a39"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "The lack of a complete theory for itinerant electron magnetism is specifically due to inadequate incorporation of orbital moments alongside spin fluctuations.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::001::introduction::2dc33c3a9e"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Accounting for longitudinal spin fluctuations enables predictions of temperature-dependent magnetic moments, fcc-hcp stability, and stacking-fault energy that are consistent with experimental observations.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::000::abstract::b3f2cb8881",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::002::results_and_discussion::13b860da4c",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::003::methods::f3287d9a39"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Conventional floating-spin calculations produce magnetic moments that depend only on volume through magnetovolume coupling.",
      "field": "baseline",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::002::results_and_discussion::13b860da4c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "The conventional floating-spin baseline neglects orbital-moment contributions.",
      "field": "baseline",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The analysis applies to both fcc and hcp CoCrFeMnNi phases.",
      "field": "boundary",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::000::abstract::b3f2cb8881",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::002::results_and_discussion::13b860da4c",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::003::methods::f3287d9a39"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Experimental information for validating finite-temperature behavior of single-phase alloys is incomplete.",
      "field": "boundary",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::000::abstract::b3f2cb8881"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Temperature-dependent XMCD measurements can directly quantify the orbital contribution and test spin-only DFT+LSF predictions.",
      "field": "falsification_experiment",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    }
  ],
